\DOC LOCAL_MUL_P25519_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_P25519_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style x86 machine-code goal past an inlined
modular-multiplication subroutine, moving the goal to a successor state
variable and recording the new result value in the assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} selecting which of the subroutine's possible
entry/variant offsets to use; the second is a {string list} giving the name of
the fresh result variable followed by the names of the two input operands whose
product is computed. The conclusion retains its {eventually} form and
postcondition, but is restated for a new, later state variable; symmetrically,
each assumption about the old state (program counter, loaded bytes, register
and memory contents, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame) is discharged and
re-added for the new state, with the {MAYCHANGE} frame extended by the
components written by the executed code. Two further assumptions are added: one
defining the named result variable as the product of the two named operand
values reduced modulo the field prime, and a memory-read fact stating that the
destination buffer holds that result. Pre-existing arithmetic/bound assumptions
are kept, sometimes in a different order.
{
   A ?- eventually x86 P s
   ========================================  LOCAL_MUL_P25519_TAC n ["u"; "t1"; "t2"]
   A' ?- eventually x86 P s'
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the state-dependent assumptions re-indexed to
{s'} and augmented by {u = (t1 * t2) MOD p} together with the corresponding
memory-read fact.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, the tactic performs exactly this single-subgoal step on
goals of this machine-code form.

\ENDDOC
